# NOETHERVIM: LANGUAGE SERVERS AND DIAGNOSTICS

A language server reads your project the way a compiler does and answers
questions about it: where is this defined, who calls it, what type is it,
rename it everywhere. This lesson is the keys for asking.

This is core. No bundle is needed, though a language bundle deepens it for
the language it covers.

Table of contents:

- [What attached, and what did not](*attach*)
- [Going places](*goto*)
- [Changing code](*change*)
- [Diagnostics](*diagnostics*)
- [Formatting](*format*)
- [Turning it off](*off*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## WHAT ATTACHED, AND WHAT DID NOT *attach*

Open a file in a language with a server available and it attaches on its
own, installing the server first if it is missing.

Two panels report on it:

    `<C-w><C-l>`   what the server is doing right now
    `<C-w><C-m>`   Mason, which installs servers, formatters and linters

The statusline shows an indicator while a server is starting or indexing. A
large project can take a while before answers are available, and a key that
does nothing in the first few seconds is usually this rather than a missing
binding.

IMPORTANT every key in this lesson is **buffer-local**, bound when a server
attaches. In a buffer with no server they do nothing, which is why this
lesson cannot make you press them here: a tutorial buffer has no server.
Open a real source file to try them.

## GOING PLACES *goto*

Navigation is under `g`, which is Vim's own prefix for going to things.

    `gd`   the definition of the symbol under the cursor
    `gD`   its declaration
    `gt`   its type definition
    `gi`   its implementations
    `gR`   references, in a floating window
    `gw`   search every symbol in the workspace by name
    `go`   an outline of this file
    `gs`   the signature of the function you are calling
    `gy`   yank the type of the thing under the cursor

`<C-o>` goes back where you came from, and `<C-i>` forward again. Those are
Vim's own jump list and work across all of these.

There are pickers for the same questions, when you would rather search a
list than jump straight there:

    `<Space>ld`   definitions
    `<Space>lr`   references
    `<Space>lo`   this file's symbols
    `<Space>lp`   the project's symbols

And two for call hierarchy: `<Space>li` for what calls this, `<Space>lO`
for what this calls.

Fill in the key that jumps to a definition:

Go to the definition under the cursor: ___

## CHANGING CODE *change*

    `<F2>`   rename this symbol everywhere, reporting how many it changed
    `<F1>`   code actions: the fixes and refactors the server offers here
    `<F4>`   the same as `<F1>`, for keyboards where `<F1>` is taken

`<F1>` in visual mode offers actions for the selection rather than the
cursor.

Rename is the one worth building a habit around. It is not a search and
replace; the server knows which occurrences of the name are this symbol and
which are a different one that happens to share it.

NOTE on macOS the function keys need `fn` held unless "Use F1, F2, etc. as
standard function keys" is on.

## DIAGNOSTICS *diagnostics*

Errors, warnings and hints arrive in the margin, inline, and in the
statusline count.

Moving between them is directional, so it is on the bracket pair:

    `]d` `[d`   next and previous diagnostic in this buffer
    `]D` `[D`   the last and first one

Reading them:

    `gl`          the full message for this line, in a float
    `<C-w>d`      the diagnostics under the cursor
    `<Space>dd`   a searchable list for this buffer
    `<Space>dp`   the same for the whole project
    `<C-w>Q`      the diagnostics panel, across every file at once

The panel is the one to reach for when you have a real list to work
through; it stays open beside the code and follows as you fix things.

Fill in the key that opens the panel across every file:

The multi-file diagnostics panel is ___

## FORMATTING *format*

`<Leader>ff` formats the buffer, or the selection in visual mode. It
prefers a real formatter for the filetype and falls back to the language
server when there is none.

Note the pair that catches people: `<Space>ff` **finds** a file and
`<Leader>ff` **formats** one. That is the namespace split doing its job;
SearchLeader never changes your buffer.

Which formatter runs for which filetype is a plugin option, so it is
changed in `lua/user/plugins/`. Remember that the list of formatters for a
filetype is an array, and merging replaces arrays rather than extending
them; see [array-valued options](noethervim-user-plugins-arrays).

## TURNING IT OFF *off*

Sometimes a server is wrong, slow, or noisy in one file.

    `]oL` `[oL`   the language server off and on
    `]oC` `[oC`   the completion menu off and on
    `]oT` `[oT`   treesitter off and on

These are per-buffer, and the completion one falls back to Neovim's native
keyword completion rather than leaving you with nothing.

